{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017rBtcnfYbRJdbojt94G6Hd","type":"tool_result","content":"   635→- All existing verification checks pass (the `shouldNotExist` and `shouldExist` lists)\n   636→\n   637→**Step 6.7: Test release dry-run (if on develop with clean tree)**\n   638→```bash\n   639→bun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run --skip-checks\n   640→```\n   641→Verify validation passes. This exercises the merge feasibility check which now includes `\"docs/\"` in `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`.\n   642→\n   643→### Phase 7: Vercel configuration (out-of-band)\n   644→\n   645→This is a manual change in the Vercel dashboard, not a code change.\n   646→\n   647→**Step 7.1: Update Vercel project settings**\n   648→\n   649→1. Go to the Vercel project for `hankweave-docs`.\n   650→2. Under \"Git\" -> \"Connected Git Repository\", change from `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`.\n   651→3. Set \"Root Directory\" to `docs`.\n   652→4. Verify the Framework Preset is still \"Next.js\".\n   653→5. The `vercel.json` at `docs/vercel.json` specifies `\"framework\": \"nextjs\"`, `\"installCommand\": \"bun install\"`, `\"buildCommand\": \"bun run build\"`, and contains a `\"build\": { \"env\": { \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\" } }` flag. This is a Vercel-specific mechanism to acknowledge deploying with a known CVE. It is being carried over verbatim from the existing docs repo, where it was a deliberate team decision. **This is a human-approval gate: the implementing agent should not proceed with Phase 7 without explicit sign-off from a team member who owns the docs Vercel deployment and knows why this flag exists.** The agent cannot make this determination on their own.\n   654→\n   655→**Step 7.2: Install behavior with `docs/` as Root Directory**\n   656→\n   657→Setting Root Directory to `docs/` means Vercel picks up `docs/vercel.json` for its configuration (installCommand, buildCommand, CVE env flag). This is important: Vercel looks for `vercel.json` at the configured Root Directory. If Root Directory were set to `/` (repo root) instead, the `docs/vercel.json` would be ignored entirely — the installCommand, buildCommand, and the CVE override flag would not apply. That is why `docs/` must be the Root Directory.\n   658→\n   659→With Root Directory set to `docs/`, Vercel checks out the full repo but sets the working directory to `docs/`. The `installCommand` (`\"bun install\"`) runs from `docs/`. Bun traverses upward from `docs/` to find the workspace root's `package.json` (which contains the `\"workspaces\"` field), then resolves and installs the full dependency tree from there. This is standard Bun workspace behavior — Bun always looks for the nearest ancestor `package.json` with a `\"workspaces\"` field.\n   660→\n   661→**If workspace resolution fails**: If `bun install` from `docs/` does not correctly find the workspace root (unlikely but not well-documented for Vercel's checkout structure), the fallback is to override `installCommand` in the Vercel dashboard to `\"cd .. && bun install\"`, which explicitly runs from the repo root. This is a dashboard-only change and does not require code modifications.\n   662→\n   663→**Alternative (repo-root approach)**: Set Root Directory to `/` and configure all commands via the Vercel dashboard (not `vercel.json`): `installCommand: \"bun install\"`, `buildCommand: \"cd docs && bun run build\"`. This guarantees workspace resolution but means `docs/vercel.json` is not used — all settings including the CVE env flag must be replicated in the Vercel dashboard. This approach is more fragile because dashboard settings are not version-controlled and the CVE flag could be silently lost.\n   664→\n   665→**Step 7.3: Configure \"Ignored Build Step\" (recommended)**\n   666→\n   667→To prevent unnecessary docs rebuilds when only runtime code changes:\n   668→1. Go to Settings -> General -> \"Ignored Build Step\"\n   669→2. Set to: `git diff --quiet HEAD^ HEAD -- docs/`\n   670→\n   671→This tells Vercel to skip the build if no files under `docs/` have changed.\n   672→\n   673→**Caveat**: Vercel may perform shallow clones for deployments. If `HEAD^` is not available in a shallow clone, this command will fail. In that case, Vercel would proceed with the build (the command exits non-zero, which Vercel treats as \"do not skip\"). This is a safe failure mode — Vercel builds unnecessarily rather than skipping a needed build. If this becomes a problem (frequent unnecessary builds), the more robust alternative is to use Vercel's environment variable approach: a custom script that checks `VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA` against the previous deployment.\n   674→\n   675→**Step 7.4: Configure branch deployment**\n   676→\n   677→The monorepo uses `develop` and `release/alpha` branches. Configure Vercel:\n   678→- Production deployment: whichever branch the docs should deploy from (likely `develop` or `release/alpha`, depending on team preference)\n   679→- Preview deployments: PRs targeting `develop`\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0699f0ac-31c0-43c3-bcfd-da3fdd996bc7","uuid":"3db0b0ff-5d61-4295-b42e-5e3e2b72a16a","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/philip/projects/hw-budget-tests/plan-gen-execution/agentRoot/plan/merged/plan.md","content":"- All existing verification checks pass (the `shouldNotExist` and `shouldExist` lists)\n\n**Step 6.7: Test release dry-run (if on develop with clean tree)**\n```bash\nbun scripts/release.ts patch --dry-run --skip-checks\n```\nVerify validation passes. This exercises the merge feasibility check which now includes `\"docs/\"` in `INTERNAL_FILES_TO_STRIP`.\n\n### Phase 7: Vercel configuration (out-of-band)\n\nThis is a manual change in the Vercel dashboard, not a code change.\n\n**Step 7.1: Update Vercel project settings**\n\n1. Go to the Vercel project for `hankweave-docs`.\n2. Under \"Git\" -> \"Connected Git Repository\", change from `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave-docs` to `SouthBridgeAI/hankweave`.\n3. Set \"Root Directory\" to `docs`.\n4. Verify the Framework Preset is still \"Next.js\".\n5. The `vercel.json` at `docs/vercel.json` specifies `\"framework\": \"nextjs\"`, `\"installCommand\": \"bun install\"`, `\"buildCommand\": \"bun run build\"`, and contains a `\"build\": { \"env\": { \"DANGEROUSLY_DEPLOY_VULNERABLE_CVE_2026_0969\": \"1\" } }` flag. This is a Vercel-specific mechanism to acknowledge deploying with a known CVE. It is being carried over verbatim from the existing docs repo, where it was a deliberate team decision. **This is a human-approval gate: the implementing agent should not proceed with Phase 7 without explicit sign-off from a team member who owns the docs Vercel deployment and knows why this flag exists.** The agent cannot make this determination on their own.\n\n**Step 7.2: Install behavior with `docs/` as Root Directory**\n\nSetting Root Directory to `docs/` means Vercel picks up `docs/vercel.json` for its configuration (installCommand, buildCommand, CVE env flag). This is important: Vercel looks for `vercel.json` at the configured Root Directory. If Root Directory were set to `/` (repo root) instead, the `docs/vercel.json` would be ignored entirely — the installCommand, buildCommand, and the CVE override flag would not apply. That is why `docs/` must be the Root Directory.\n\nWith Root Directory set to `docs/`, Vercel checks out the full repo but sets the working directory to `docs/`. The `installCommand` (`\"bun install\"`) runs from `docs/`. Bun traverses upward from `docs/` to find the workspace root's `package.json` (which contains the `\"workspaces\"` field), then resolves and installs the full dependency tree from there. This is standard Bun workspace behavior — Bun always looks for the nearest ancestor `package.json` with a `\"workspaces\"` field.\n\n**If workspace resolution fails**: If `bun install` from `docs/` does not correctly find the workspace root (unlikely but not well-documented for Vercel's checkout structure), the fallback is to override `installCommand` in the Vercel dashboard to `\"cd .. && bun install\"`, which explicitly runs from the repo root. This is a dashboard-only change and does not require code modifications.\n\n**Alternative (repo-root approach)**: Set Root Directory to `/` and configure all commands via the Vercel dashboard (not `vercel.json`): `installCommand: \"bun install\"`, `buildCommand: \"cd docs && bun run build\"`. This guarantees workspace resolution but means `docs/vercel.json` is not used — all settings including the CVE env flag must be replicated in the Vercel dashboard. This approach is more fragile because dashboard settings are not version-controlled and the CVE flag could be silently lost.\n\n**Step 7.3: Configure \"Ignored Build Step\" (recommended)**\n\nTo prevent unnecessary docs rebuilds when only runtime code changes:\n1. Go to Settings -> General -> \"Ignored Build Step\"\n2. Set to: `git diff --quiet HEAD^ HEAD -- docs/`\n\nThis tells Vercel to skip the build if no files under `docs/` have changed.\n\n**Caveat**: Vercel may perform shallow clones for deployments. If `HEAD^` is not available in a shallow clone, this command will fail. In that case, Vercel would proceed with the build (the command exits non-zero, which Vercel treats as \"do not skip\"). This is a safe failure mode — Vercel builds unnecessarily rather than skipping a needed build. If this becomes a problem (frequent unnecessary builds), the more robust alternative is to use Vercel's environment variable approach: a custom script that checks `VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA` against the previous deployment.\n\n**Step 7.4: Configure branch deployment**\n\nThe monorepo uses `develop` and `release/alpha` branches. Configure Vercel:\n- Production deployment: whichever branch the docs should deploy from (likely `develop` or `release/alpha`, depending on team preference)\n- Preview deployments: PRs targeting `develop`","numLines":45,"startLine":635,"totalLines":891}},"timestamp":"2026-03-05T13:13:20.734Z"}
